# DevOps Practices

Directives for CI/CD, containers, orchestration, IaC, and observability. Tool names are examples; apply the equivalent in your platform.

## CI/CD pipelines

- Stages: lint/type-check/test → build → deploy; never deploy without passing tests and build
- Fail on lint, test, or high/critical dependency-audit findings; run secret scanning on every push
- Build once, promote the same artefact through environments; tag images with the commit SHA, never only `latest`
- Deploy only from protected branches with environment approvals; verify rollout (e.g. `kubectl rollout status`) and roll back automatically on failure
- Cache dependencies; use matrix builds only where multiple versions matter
- Keep pipelines fast: parallelise, run expensive suites on PR/merge rather than every commit

## Pipeline security

- Pin third-party actions/steps to a full commit SHA; review updates (e.g. via Dependabot/Renovate)
- Grant the pipeline token minimal permissions (e.g. `permissions: contents: read` by default)
- Use OIDC workload identity to cloud providers instead of long-lived secrets
- Never run untrusted PR code with secrets (beware `pull_request_target`-style triggers)
- Generate an SBOM and build provenance/attestations; sign images (e.g. Sigstore/cosign)

## Containers

- Multi-stage builds: build tooling stays in the builder stage; copy only artefacts into a minimal runtime image (slim, distroless, or alpine)
- Pin base images to a supported version (ideally by digest); rebuild regularly for security patches
- Run as a non-root user; `COPY --chown`; no secrets in layers or build args (use build secrets)
- Use `.dockerignore`; install production dependencies only in the runtime stage
- Scan images in CI and fail on high/critical vulnerabilities

```dockerfile
FROM node:22-slim AS builder
WORKDIR /app
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm ci
COPY . .
RUN npm run build && npm prune --omit=dev

FROM node:22-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=builder --chown=node:node /app/node_modules ./node_modules
COPY --from=builder --chown=node:node /app/dist ./dist
USER node
CMD ["node", "dist/index.js"]
```

## Compose (local and small deployments)

- One service per concern (app, db, cache, queue, proxy); named volumes for persistent data
- `depends_on` with `condition: service_healthy` and real health checks; do not rely on start order
- Secrets via `.env` (gitignored) or secrets files; never hardcoded

## Kubernetes

- Set resource requests (and memory limits); use `RollingUpdate` with sensible `maxSurge`/`maxUnavailable`
- Define readiness, liveness, and (for slow starts) startup probes; liveness must not depend on downstream services
- Security context: `runAsNonRoot`, `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`, `readOnlyRootFilesystem: true`, drop all capabilities; `emptyDir` for writable paths
- Config in ConfigMaps; credentials from an external secret store (e.g. External Secrets, CSI driver) — Kubernetes Secrets are only base64-encoded, not encrypted
- Apply NetworkPolicies (default deny), PodDisruptionBudgets for critical services, and Pod Security Standards (`restricted`)
- Ingress/Gateway with TLS; automate certificates (e.g. cert-manager)

```yaml
securityContext: { runAsNonRoot: true, allowPrivilegeEscalation: false, readOnlyRootFilesystem: true, capabilities: { drop: [ALL] } }
resources: { requests: { cpu: "100m", memory: "128Mi" }, limits: { memory: "256Mi" } }
readinessProbe: { httpGet: { path: /ready, port: 3000 } }
livenessProbe: { httpGet: { path: /health, port: 3000 } }
```

## Infrastructure as Code (e.g. Terraform/OpenTofu, Bicep, Pulumi)

- Pin tool, provider, and module versions; commit lock files
- Remote state with locking and encryption; separate state per environment; never commit state files
- Variables for environment-specific values; small, focused modules; consistent tagging
- Review the plan/what-if output before apply; apply from CI, not laptops
- Scan IaC for misconfiguration (e.g. Checkov, Trivy, tfsec) and detect drift

## Monitoring and alerting

- Instrument with OpenTelemetry (traces, metrics, logs) and export to your backend
- Track the golden signals: request rate, error rate, latency (p50/p95/p99), saturation; plus dependency health
- Define SLOs and alert on SLO burn rate rather than raw thresholds; route alerts to an owner with a runbook
- Avoid alert fatigue: every alert must be actionable

## Logging

- Structured (JSON) logs with timestamp, level, message, service, and trace/correlation IDs
- Never log secrets or sensitive data; redact at the sink
- Centralise logs; set retention per environment and compliance needs

## Checklist

- [ ] Pipeline: tests gate build and deploy; artefact built once and promoted
- [ ] Supply chain: actions pinned by SHA, minimal token permissions, OIDC, SBOM, signed images
- [ ] Containers: multi-stage, minimal pinned base, non-root, scanned
- [ ] Kubernetes: probes, requests/limits, restricted security context, NetworkPolicies, external secrets
- [ ] IaC: pinned versions, remote locked state, reviewed plans, misconfiguration scanning
- [ ] Observability: OpenTelemetry, SLO-based alerts with runbooks, structured logs without sensitive data
